1
00:00:00,206 --> 00:00:08,960
Right now, while you watch this, a piece of software is unpacking this video for you, frame by frame, about 30 times a second.

2
00:00:09,582 --> 00:00:15,800
On a lot of phones and computers, the part doing the heaviest work wasn't written in Python or Java or even C.

3
00:00:16,398 --> 00:00:23,460
A person wrote it by hand, one instruction at a time, in a language that was first described on paper in 1947.

4
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:25,920
That's older than the transistor.

5
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:30,600
It's older than the first computer that ever ran a program stored in its own memory.

6
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:42,580
And the person who wrote it down was a 25-year-old mathematician from the English Midlands, who was also building the machine she was writing for, out of the same clicking switches that telephone exchanges used.

7
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:44,560
The language is called assembly.

8
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:48,140
Programmers have been calling it dead since the 1950s.

9
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:54,340
The whole point of Fortran, back in 1957, was that nobody would ever have to write it again.

10
00:00:54,766 --> 00:01:01,860
Most universities barely teach it now, and plenty of working programmers will get through an entire career without writing a single line.

11
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:03,420
And it's still here.

12
00:01:03,854 --> 00:01:07,620
It was the first thing your computer ran when you pressed the power button this morning.

13
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:17,600
It's running on a spacecraft more than 15 billion miles from Earth, and in 2024 NASA engineers had to patch that code from the ground, piece by piece.

14
00:01:18,254 --> 00:01:27,840
And in January 2025, when a small Chinese AI lab set off the biggest one-day loss in stock market history, people went digging through its technical report.

15
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:36,820
One of the things they found was that for some of the hardest parts, its engineers had gone underneath the normal tools and written instructions in an assembly-like language.

16
00:01:37,518 --> 00:01:42,220
So how does a language from 1947 end up inside nearly every device on earth?

17
00:01:42,702 --> 00:01:47,160
And why do the people building the most advanced software in the world keep going back to it?

18
00:01:47,758 --> 00:01:49,440
This is Parts That Changed History.

19
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:56,820
This story starts on an ocean liner crossing the Atlantic in the winter of 1947, and it ends in the phone in your pocket.

20
00:01:57,454 --> 00:02:01,560
To see why assembly matters, you have to know what a processor actually understands.

21
00:02:02,222 --> 00:02:03,120
It isn't words.

22
00:02:03,598 --> 00:02:04,360
It's numbers.

23
00:02:05,102 --> 00:02:07,840
Every chip has a short list of things it knows how to do.

24
00:02:08,302 --> 00:02:15,400
Copy a number out of memory, add two numbers together, compare them, jump to a different instruction if the answer came out negative.

25
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:20,900
Each of those operations has a code, and the code is a pattern of ones and zeros.

26
00:02:21,582 --> 00:02:23,960
In the earliest computers, that was the whole job.

27
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:35,800
If you wanted the machine to add two numbers, you looked up the binary pattern for ADD in a table, then the pattern for the memory address of the first number, then the second, and you wrote them all down.

28
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:37,480
Then you checked them.

29
00:02:37,806 --> 00:02:39,080
Then you checked them to digit.

30
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:43,120
Again, because one wrong digit didn't give you an error message.

31
00:02:44,014 --> 00:02:48,440
It gave the machine a different instruction, and the machine would run it without complaint.

32
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:50,820
Assembly is a very small idea.

33
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:54,920
Instead of writing the pattern for ADD, you write ADD.

34
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:59,440
Instead of a memory address in binary, you write a name, like TOTAL.

35
00:03:00,110 --> 00:03:04,620
A short program called an assembler reads your words and swaps each one for its number.

36
00:03:05,486 --> 00:03:07,100
That's really all it is.

37
00:03:07,598 --> 00:03:10,660
One line of assembly is one instruction for the chip.

38
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:13,580
There's no hidden layer between you and the hardware.

39
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,840
What you write is what the processor does, in the order you wrote it.

40
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:28,520
And every other language you've heard of, Python, Java, C, Rust, eventually has to be turned into this, or into the numbers it stands for, before a processor can run it.

41
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:32,780
There are five good reasons assembly should have disappeared a long time ago.

42
00:03:33,390 --> 00:03:35,580
The first is that it was built to be replaced.

43
00:03:36,142 --> 00:03:39,020
The people who used it every day hated it.

44
00:03:39,566 --> 00:03:46,080
If you watched our Fortran video, you'll remember John Backus calling that kind of programming hand-to-hand combat with the machine.

45
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:50,040
Fortran existed so that scientists could stop writing assembly.

46
00:03:50,766 --> 00:03:52,200
The second is the compilers.

47
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:55,340
For decades, the argument for assembly was speed.

48
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,720
A skilled human could beat the machine.

49
00:03:58,670 --> 00:04:05,680
Then compilers got good, and today a modern compiler will usually produce faster code than most people could write by hand.

50
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:08,040
The third is that it doesn't travel.

51
00:04:08,558 --> 00:04:12,600
Assembly written for an Intel chip is useless on the chip inside an iPhone.

52
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:21,180
Every family of processors has its own instructions, so every time the hardware changes, the code has to be written again from scratch.

53
00:04:21,902 --> 00:04:23,460
The fourth is that it's unforgiving.

54
00:04:23,854 --> 00:04:25,080
There are no guardrails.

55
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:35,000
Assembly will let you overwrite your own program, read memory you have no business reading, and crash the whole machine, and it won't warn you about any of it.

56
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:36,860
And the fifth is the people.

57
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:40,660
The generation that learned to program in Assembly is retiring.

58
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:49,380
On the Tyobi Index, one of the best-known rankings of programming languages, Assembly was in the top 10 as recently as 2023.

59
00:04:50,222 --> 00:04:53,860
By the end of 2025, it had slid down to 19th.

60
00:04:54,382 --> 00:04:56,100
Any one of those should have been enough.

61
00:04:56,494 --> 00:05:00,500
To see why none of them was, you have to start with the woman who wrote it down first.

62
00:05:00,782 --> 00:05:07,640
Kathleen Britton was born on July 9, 1922, in Storbridge, a small town near Birmingham.

63
00:05:08,462 --> 00:05:17,900
She studied mathematics at the University of London, and from 1944 she worked as a junior scientific officer at the Royal Aircraft Establishment in Farnborough.

64
00:05:18,510 --> 00:05:26,400
In 1946, she took a job as a research assistant at Birkbeck College in London, working for a physicist named Andrew Booth.

65
00:05:26,958 --> 00:05:28,120
Booth studied crystals.

66
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:36,220
Working out the shape of a crystal from x-ray photographs meant an enormous amount of arithmetic, and he wanted a machine to do it for him.

67
00:05:36,878 --> 00:05:42,040
So the two of them, along with another research assistant named Xenia Sweeting, started building one.

68
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:45,380
They called it the ARC, the Automatic Relay Calculator.

69
00:05:46,020 --> 00:05:50,260
It was made from relays, little electrical switches that click open and shut.

70
00:05:50,798 --> 00:05:56,880
The Birkbeck group was the smallest of the early British computer teams, and Kathleen didn't just program the thing.

71
00:05:57,390 --> 00:05:59,380
She built a large part of it herself.

72
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:06,980
On February 28, 1947, Kathleen Britton and Andrew Booth sailed from Southampton on the Queen Elizabeth.

73
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:15,520
They reached New York on March 5, and went on to the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, paid for by the Rockefeller Foundation.

74
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:22,880
That's where John von Neumann was working on a new kind of computer, one that kept its program in the same memory as its data.

75
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:26,000
It was the first time the two of them had heard of the idea.

76
00:06:26,734 --> 00:06:28,340
That visit changed their plans.

77
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:34,280
They decided to redesign their machine around von Neumann's design, and that year they wrote up two reports.

78
00:06:35,022 --> 00:06:37,500
The second one came out in September 1947.

79
00:06:38,254 --> 00:06:40,220
It was called Coding for ARC.

80
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:46,100
In it, Kathleen laid out a way to write the machine's instructions as short symbols instead of raw binary.

81
00:06:46,766 --> 00:06:50,600
It's generally considered the first published description of an assembly language.

82
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:53,680
She also wrote the assembler that went with it.

83
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:54,700
She was 25.

84
00:06:55,534 --> 00:07:01,500
For a sense of how early that was, in September 1947 the transistor didn't exist yet.

85
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:05,120
Bell Labs wouldn't demonstrate the first one until three months later.

86
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:10,620
There wasn't a single computer anywhere in the world that had run a program stored in its own memory.

87
00:07:11,406 --> 00:07:14,740
That wouldn't happen until June 1948 in Manchester.

88
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:20,240
Kathleen Booth was describing how to program a kind of machine that nobody had finished building.

89
00:07:20,860 --> 00:07:25,000
She married Andrew in 1950, the same year she earned her PhD.

90
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:32,840
Between 1947 and 1953, the Birkbeck team built three machines, and Kathleen wrote the programs for them.

91
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:37,680
In 1957, the Booths helped found Birkbeck's Department of Computing.

92
00:07:38,126 --> 00:07:42,580
In 1958, she published one of the first books on how to program a computer.

93
00:07:43,214 --> 00:07:51,560
Writing down a notation is one thing, Getting people to work that way every day is another, and that part happened about 60 miles away, in Cambridge.

94
00:07:52,398 --> 00:07:58,980
On May 6, 1949, a machine called EDSAC ran its first program at the University of Cambridge.

95
00:07:59,662 --> 00:08:07,660
It was built by a team led by Maurice Wilkes, and it became the first computer in the world to offer a regular computing service to people who hadn't built it.

96
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,400
Wilkes had the same problem every early computer builder had.

97
00:08:11,822 --> 00:08:14,800
When you switched EDSAC on, its memory was empty.

98
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:19,140
So how do you load a program into a machine that doesn't yet know how to read one?

99
00:08:19,694 --> 00:08:22,800
He gave the problem to a research student named David Wheeler.

100
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:26,840
Wheeler's answer was a tiny program called the Initial Orders.

101
00:08:27,470 --> 00:08:34,500
It was wired permanently into a bank of telephone stepping switches, and every time the machine started, those switches put it into memory.

102
00:08:35,214 --> 00:08:37,660
The first version was 31 instructions long.

103
00:08:38,094 --> 00:08:45,620
It read a program off paper tape, written with letters for the instructions, turned each one into binary, and placed it in memory.

104
00:08:46,254 --> 00:08:52,900
It's usually described as the world's first working assembler, and it solved the startup problem at the same time.

105
00:08:53,422 --> 00:08:58,860
That September, Wheeler replaced it with a second version that did a lot more, in 41 instructions.

106
00:08:59,502 --> 00:09:02,560
People punched their programs onto paper tape, in assembly.

107
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:08,840
After a while, some of them got good enough to hold a tape up to the light and read the code straight off the holes.

108
00:09:09,518 --> 00:09:18,780
Wheeler went on to earn the world's first PhD in computer science, and he's credited with inventing the subroutine, a block of code you can jump into and come back from.

109
00:09:19,406 --> 00:09:22,460
Nearly every program written since uses that idea.

110
00:09:23,118 --> 00:09:25,360
But keep in mind what those initial orders were.

111
00:09:25,966 --> 00:09:29,100
They were the very first thing the machine ran when you turned it on.

112
00:09:29,646 --> 00:09:31,320
That detail comes back later.

113
00:09:32,078 --> 00:09:35,380
Through the 1950s, assembly basically was programming.

114
00:09:35,918 --> 00:09:40,620
If you wanted a computer to do something, you wrote it in assembly, or in raw numbers.

115
00:09:41,358 --> 00:09:43,780
That's exactly what Fortran was built to end.

116
00:09:44,238 --> 00:09:55,640
When IBM released it in 1957, the big fear was that code written by a compiler would be slow, and on a computer that rented for a fortune, slow code was wasted money.

117
00:09:56,238 --> 00:10:02,100
The Fortran team spent most of its effort making the compiler produce code nearly as good as a skilled humans.

118
00:10:02,580 --> 00:10:06,020
They mostly pulled it off and high-level languages started to take over.

119
00:10:06,780 --> 00:10:09,280
But a certain kind of programmer never gave in.

120
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:15,140
In 1983 a humor piece called Real Programmers Don't Use Pascal made the rounds.

121
00:10:15,598 --> 00:10:21,280
It made fun of anyone who needed a friendly language and it said real programmers write in Fortran.

122
00:10:21,934 --> 00:10:31,820
On May 21st 1983 an astronomer at the University of Texas named Ed Nather posted a reply on Usenet, one of the early Internet's discussion networks.

123
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:35,980
He was basically saying, you think Fortran programmers are tough?

124
00:10:36,558 --> 00:10:39,880
It became one of the most famous stories in the history of programming.

125
00:10:40,494 --> 00:10:41,960
It's called the story of Mel.

126
00:10:42,606 --> 00:10:44,040
Mel was a real person.

127
00:10:44,430 --> 00:10:45,560
His name was Mel K.

128
00:10:46,190 --> 00:10:52,300
And in the late 1950s, he worked at Royal McBee, a typewriter company that had started making computers.

129
00:10:53,070 --> 00:10:59,480
Mel had written the most popular program the company owned, a blackjack game for its LGP-30 computer.

130
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:09,100
At trade shows, the LGP-30 booth was packed with people playing cards against the machine, and the IBM salesmen stood around, talking to each other.

131
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:17,060
When Royal McBee built a newer machine, the RPC-4000, Mel's job was to rewrite the blackjack game for it.

132
00:11:17,486 --> 00:11:25,040
Both computers kept their programs on a spinning magnetic drum, and Mel didn't trust anyone else to decide where his instructions went.

133
00:11:25,774 --> 00:11:32,120
He placed each one himself so that just as one instruction finished, the next was coming around under the read head.

134
00:11:32,654 --> 00:11:35,200
He was timing his code to the rotation of the drum.

135
00:11:35,854 --> 00:11:38,160
Then the sales department asked for a change.

136
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:42,840
They wanted a switch on the front panel that would change the odds so the customer would win.

137
00:11:43,438 --> 00:11:46,220
Mel thought that was dishonest, and he said no.

138
00:11:46,766 --> 00:11:52,020
When he was finally pushed into doing it, he added the switch, but he got the test backwards.

139
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:56,800
When you flipped it on, the computer cheated and won every hand.

140
00:11:57,380 --> 00:12:01,220
Mel left the company, and Ed Nather was told to fix the switch.

141
00:12:01,902 --> 00:12:05,240
When he went into Mel's code, he found a loop with no way out.

142
00:12:05,614 --> 00:12:08,380
There was no test to end it and no jump to leave it.

143
00:12:08,686 --> 00:12:10,780
And the program still worked.

144
00:12:11,438 --> 00:12:13,500
It took him a long time to understand why.

145
00:12:13,998 --> 00:12:18,640
Every time through the loop, the code added one to the address inside one of its own instructions.

146
00:12:19,886 --> 00:12:26,080
Eventually, the number got so big that it spilled over into the part of the instruction that said what kind of instruction it was.

147
00:12:26,734 --> 00:12:31,140
At that moment, the instruction turned itself into a jump, and the program left the loop.

148
00:12:31,780 --> 00:12:33,480
Nather decided not to touch it.

149
00:12:33,870 --> 00:12:37,580
He wrote that he didn't feel comfortable hacking up the code of a real programmer.

150
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:38,740
Mel K.

151
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:40,020
died in 2018.

152
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:45,060
People still pass his story around, and it says a lot about what the assembly loyalists were defending.

153
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:47,920
It had less to do with speed than with control.

154
00:12:48,494 --> 00:12:51,820
In assembly, nothing stands between you and the machine.

155
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:59,080
If you've ever counted clock cycles on an 8-bit chip, or you just like this kind of story, tap the like button.

156
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:01,320
It helps the channel more than you'd think.

157
00:13:01,966 --> 00:13:09,540
And later in this video, there's a computer 15 billion miles away, a dead memory chip, and a repair job with no modern tools at all.

158
00:13:10,126 --> 00:13:14,000
For decades after Fortran, assembly kept running the world anyway.

159
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:18,980
On computers with almost no memory, there often wasn't room for anything else.

160
00:13:19,694 --> 00:13:26,280
The Apollo Guidance Computer, the one that landed Apollo 11 on the moon, was programmed in its own assembly language.

161
00:13:26,926 --> 00:13:34,212
We went through that code in our last video, including a comment in the landing equations that reads, temporary, I hope, hope, hope.

162
00:13:34,798 --> 00:13:36,140
It flew to the moon anyway.

163
00:13:36,980 --> 00:13:45,640
In 1969, at Bell Labs, Ken Thompson wrote the first version of UNIX in assembly, on a spare PDP-7 minicomputer.

164
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:50,460
A few years later, he and Dennis Ritchie rewrote most of it in their new language.

165
00:13:51,054 --> 00:13:55,500
See, that rewrite usually gets told as the moment assembly lost.

166
00:13:56,078 --> 00:14:00,820
A whole operating system in a language that could be moved from one machine to another.

167
00:14:01,486 --> 00:14:07,800
Unix and the systems built in its image, including Linux, Android, and macOS, grew out of that decision.

168
00:14:08,494 --> 00:14:09,780
But the story didn't end there.

169
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:17,920
When IBM launched its PC in 1981, the operating system, MS-DOS, was written in 8086 assembly.

170
00:14:18,580 --> 00:14:22,280
Microsoft has since released the source code for several early versions.

171
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:29,480
In April 2024, it published the code for MS-DOS 4.0, and nearly all of it is assembly.

172
00:14:30,190 --> 00:14:40,700
A whole generation of home computers and game consoles ran on chips like the 6502 and the Z80, and the games on them were written in assembly, because nothing else would fit.

173
00:14:41,230 --> 00:14:46,660
And in 1999, a Scottish programmer named Chris Sawyer released Roller Coaster Tycoon.

174
00:14:47,182 --> 00:14:51,560
By his own account, about 99% of it was written in x86 assembly.

175
00:14:52,366 --> 00:14:58,564
A whole theme park, thousands of little guests, each with their own wants and moods, running smoothly on an ordinary home PC.

176
00:14:59,566 --> 00:15:03,680
Still, by the 2000s, the five reasons from earlier had mostly won.

177
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:07,220
Compilers were excellent, and hardware was cheap.

178
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:10,700
Almost nobody wrote whole programs in assembly anymore.

179
00:15:11,420 --> 00:15:15,140
So why does the title of this video say it's inside every computer?

180
00:15:16,060 --> 00:15:17,720
Compilers didn't replace assembly.

181
00:15:18,254 --> 00:15:19,300
They automated it.

182
00:15:19,822 --> 00:15:30,900
When you build a program in C or Rust or Swift, the compiler's job is to turn your code into machine instructions, and a lot of compilers do that by writing assembly first and handing it to an assembler.

183
00:15:31,566 --> 00:15:35,220
You can ask most of them to stop halfway and show you the assembly they produced.

184
00:15:35,840 --> 00:15:39,740
Programmers do it all the time when they want to see what the machine is really being told to do.

185
00:15:40,270 --> 00:15:44,840
Even Python runs on an interpreter that was itself compiled down to machine instructions.

186
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:58,240
Every program on your computer, whatever language it started in, ends up as the same kind of thing Kathleen Booth was describing in 1947, a list of numbered instructions for the chip, with names a person can read.

187
00:15:59,118 --> 00:16:03,940
And there are still four places where people write it by hand, because nothing else will do.

188
00:16:04,590 --> 00:16:07,120
The first is the moment you turn the machine on.

189
00:16:07,694 --> 00:16:10,580
When a processor wakes up, almost nothing is ready.

190
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:17,360
Memory hasn't been set up, there's no stack, the scratch space a language like C needs just to call a function.

191
00:16:18,190 --> 00:16:22,940
So the very first instructions have to be written for the bare chip, and they're written in assembly.

192
00:16:23,694 --> 00:16:34,640
On a typical Intel or AMD computer, the processor starts by jumping to one fixed address near the top of its memory space, and the code waiting there was written for exactly that moment.

193
00:16:35,438 --> 00:16:39,600
Linux has its own handwritten assembly at the very start of its boot process.

194
00:16:40,142 --> 00:16:41,400
Phones work the same way.

195
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:47,380
That's David Wheeler's problem from 1949, how you get a machine that knows nothing to start running.

196
00:16:47,982 --> 00:16:51,360
77 years later, the answer is still the same kind of code.

197
00:16:51,980 --> 00:16:55,300
The second place is wherever speed matters more than anything else.

198
00:16:56,580 --> 00:17:00,500
FFmpeg is a free, open-source program for handling video and audio.

199
00:17:00,974 --> 00:17:06,800
You've almost certainly used it without knowing, because it's buried inside a huge number of apps and services.

200
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:10,420
Its developers still write parts of it by hand in assembly.

201
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:25,740
In November 2024, they posted a benchmark showing one function, rewritten in hand-tuned assembly for a newer set of Intel and AMD instructions called AVX-512, running up to 94 times faster than the plain C version.

202
00:17:26,510 --> 00:17:28,132
That number needs a little honesty.

203
00:17:28,846 --> 00:17:37,180
It was one small function, not the whole program, RAM, and compared with the older handwritten assembly it replaced, the gain was much smaller.

204
00:17:37,902 --> 00:17:43,140
But it made the point, when engineers really need the speed, they still drop down to this layer.

205
00:17:43,790 --> 00:17:44,660
Then there's David.

206
00:17:45,070 --> 00:17:50,460
It's a decoder for AV1, the video format YouTube and Netflix use to save bandwidth.

207
00:17:51,214 --> 00:18:00,640
It was built by the people behind VLC and FFmpeg, and it contains more than 200,000 lines of assembly, written separately for different families of chips.

208
00:18:01,326 --> 00:18:04,180
Chrome switched to it in 2019, and so did Firefox.

209
00:18:04,814 --> 00:18:11,500
In 2024, Google brought it to Android phones so they could play AV1 video and software, and YouTube uses it.

210
00:18:11,886 --> 00:18:13,860
Which brings us back to the start of this video.

211
00:18:14,478 --> 00:18:25,500
If you're watching on a device that doesn't have a dedicated AV1 chip, there's a decent chance the frames on your screen right now are being unpacked by code that somebody wrote by hand, one instruction at a time.

212
00:18:26,094 --> 00:18:27,580
The third place is security.

213
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:36,660
When your phone encrypts your messages, or talks to your bank's website, the math has to be fast, and it has to be careful in a way most code isn't.

214
00:18:37,230 --> 00:18:46,180
If an encryption routine takes slightly longer for some secret keys than for others, an attacker who can time it precisely may be able to work backwards toward the key.

215
00:18:46,180 --> 00:18:53,040
So the libraries that do this work, like OpenSSL, carry handwritten assembly for their most sensitive routines.

216
00:18:53,820 --> 00:19:01,940
The programmer needs to know exactly which instructions run and how long each one takes, and assembly is the most direct way to guarantee that.

217
00:19:02,382 --> 00:19:04,068
The fourth place is the strangest.

218
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:07,920
It's machines nobody can replace, because nobody can reach them.

219
00:19:08,526 --> 00:19:10,980
Voyager 1 launched in 1977.

220
00:19:11,880 --> 00:19:18,120
It's now more than 15 billion miles from Earth, farther away than anything else people have ever built.

221
00:19:18,830 --> 00:19:22,420
Its onboard computers were programmed in assembly, and they still are.

222
00:19:23,022 --> 00:19:27,820
On November 14th, 2023, Voyager 1 stopped making sense.

223
00:19:28,302 --> 00:19:33,420
It was still sending a signal home, but the data inside it was gibberish.

224
00:19:34,062 --> 00:19:38,400
It took engineers at NASA's Jet Propulsion laboratory months to work out why.

225
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:47,860
In March 2024, they sent a command that told the spacecraft's flight data subsystem to send back a readout of its own memory, and in it they found the problem.

226
00:19:48,462 --> 00:19:55,900
A single chip, holding about 3% of that computer's memory, had failed, and some of the computer's own code had been stored on it.

227
00:19:56,526 --> 00:20:02,060
They couldn't replace the chip, and there wasn't one empty space big enough to hold the code that had been lost.

228
00:20:02,060 --> 00:20:13,660
So, they cut it into pieces, found small, unused gaps scattered around the memory, rewrote each piece to fit, and updated every reference so the pieces would still work together.

229
00:20:14,350 --> 00:20:22,360
The assembly language was documented, but the original development tools and test hardware were long gone, so a lot of that work had to be done by hand.

230
00:20:22,926 --> 00:20:25,940
On April 18, 2024, they sent the first piece.

231
00:20:26,478 --> 00:20:31,760
The radio signal took about 22 and a half hours to reach Voyager, and just as long to come back.

232
00:20:32,302 --> 00:20:36,460
On April 20th, the reply arrived, and this time, it made sense.

233
00:20:37,038 --> 00:20:41,240
By June, all four of its working science instruments were sending data home again.

234
00:20:41,806 --> 00:20:50,580
More than three quarters of a century after coding for ARC, people were still writing assembly by hand, for a computer none of them will ever see.

235
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:55,300
The newest example comes from AI, and almost nobody expected it.

236
00:20:55,960 --> 00:21:04,360
In late December 2024, a Chinese company called DeepSeek published a technical report on its new AI model, V3.

237
00:21:05,006 --> 00:21:15,540
It said the model had been trained on 2,048 NVIDIA H800 chips in about two months, a fraction of the hardware the big American labs were throwing at the same problem.

238
00:21:16,174 --> 00:21:19,040
A few weeks later, its R1 model went viral.

239
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:30,200
On January 27th, 2025, NVIDIA's stock fell about 17%, wiping out roughly $589 billion of market value in a single day.

240
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:34,240
No company had ever lost that much in one trading session.

241
00:21:35,054 --> 00:21:40,540
People went through DeepSeek's report to figure out how they'd done it, and one detail got a lot of attention.

242
00:21:41,198 --> 00:21:45,840
The H800 was a cut-down chip, made to comply with American export rules.

243
00:21:46,126 --> 00:21:53,980
To get more out of it, the team took 20 of each chip's 132 processing units and set them aside just for moving data between servers.

244
00:21:54,700 --> 00:22:04,500
And for some of that work, they went underneath CUDA, the standard way almost everyone programs NVIDIA's chips, and wrote customized instructions in something called PTX.

245
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:06,880
PTX isn't quite assembly.

246
00:22:07,470 --> 00:22:12,680
It's a layer just above the chip's real machine code, which NVIDIA keeps mostly to itself.

247
00:22:12,680 --> 00:22:20,400
But it's close enough that most of the coverage called it assembly-like, and the principal is the one Mel-K understood on a spinning drum.

248
00:22:21,038 --> 00:22:29,620
When your hardware is limited and you need every last bit of performance, you stop letting the tools decide and you tell the machine exactly what to do.

249
00:22:30,286 --> 00:22:31,600
There's an irony in it, too.

250
00:22:32,142 --> 00:22:36,760
Part of the panic that day came from the idea that DeepSeek had found a way around NVIDIA.

251
00:22:37,102 --> 00:22:42,620
Writing code in PTX ties you more tightly to NVIDIA's hardware, not less.

252
00:22:43,342 --> 00:22:46,580
Kathleen Booth spent most of her career a long way from the spotlight.

253
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:54,560
She and Andrew later moved to Canada, where she kept teaching and doing research, including work on getting machines to recognize patterns.

254
00:22:55,342 --> 00:22:59,180
For most of her life, very few people outside computing knew her name.

255
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:02,480
She died on September 29, 2022.

256
00:23:03,214 --> 00:23:04,640
She was 100 years old.

257
00:23:05,294 --> 00:23:13,680
That same year, the language she first described in a report from Princeton was still ranked among the 10 most popular programming languages in the world.

258
00:23:14,318 --> 00:23:18,720
Think back to the start of this video, and the software unpacking these frames right now.

259
00:23:19,260 --> 00:23:29,380
Underneath the app, underneath the browser and the operating system, every layer eventually turns into the same thing, a list of small, numbered steps for the chip.

260
00:23:29,902 --> 00:23:30,400
Load.

261
00:23:30,894 --> 00:23:31,320
Add.

262
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:32,320
Compare.

263
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:33,260
Jump.

264
00:23:33,966 --> 00:23:38,260
So why is a programming language from 1947 still inside every computer?

265
00:23:38,862 --> 00:23:41,760
Partly because it was never one language in the usual sense.

266
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:47,360
Assembly is the last layer a human can still read before the machine takes over.

267
00:23:48,238 --> 00:23:54,380
Every language that came after it, including the ones built to replace it, is a more comfortable way of producing it.

268
00:23:54,862 --> 00:23:55,880
Fortran did that.

269
00:23:56,398 --> 00:23:57,040
C does it.

270
00:23:57,422 --> 00:23:59,540
So does everything that runs on top of C.

271
00:23:59,540 --> 00:24:05,160
Most of the time, a compiler writes the assembly for us now, and it does a very good job.

272
00:24:05,800 --> 00:24:22,940
But when a computer first wakes up, when a video has to play smoothly on a cheap phone, when a secret has to stay secret, or when the only machine that can do the job is 15 billion miles away, somebody still has to sit down and tell the machine exactly what to do, one instruction at a time.

273
00:24:23,598 --> 00:24:31,060
That's what Kathleen Booth wrote down in 1947, at 25, for a machine she was still building with her own hands.

274
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:33,100
Now I want to hear from you.

275
00:24:33,518 --> 00:24:36,320
What was the first processor you ever wrote assembly for?

276
00:24:36,942 --> 00:24:40,380
A 6502 on an Apple II or a Commodore 64?

277
00:24:41,006 --> 00:24:47,180
A Z80, an 8086, IBM 360 assembler at work, or something stranger?

278
00:24:47,950 --> 00:24:52,500
And if you ever held a paper tape up to the light to read it, tell me about that too.

279
00:24:53,140 --> 00:24:56,740
And if I got a date, a number, or a name wrong, tell me in the comments.

280
00:24:57,200 --> 00:24:59,460
I read every one, and I pin corrections.

